+# Forking Base Locally
+
+The old onboarding note says:
+
+```sh
+yarn fork base
+cast chain-id --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+That note proves the local node answers, but it does not prove the fork is Base.
+
+## What went wrong
+
+In this repo, the root script is only a wrapper:
+
+```json
+"fork": "yarn foundry:fork",
+"foundry:fork": "yarn workspace @se-2/foundry fork"
+```
+
+The Foundry workspace script reads the network from shell positional argument `$1`, and falls back to `mainnet` when `$1` is empty:
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}"
+```
+
+The Makefile then starts Anvil with whatever `FORK_URL` was set to, but always gives the local fork chain id `31337`:
+
+```make
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+The Base RPC alias exists in `packages/foundry/foundry.toml`:
+
+```toml
+mainnet = "https://eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io/v2/${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}"
+base = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+With this Yarn script shape, `yarn fork base` does not populate `$1` for the workspace script. A dry run shows the problem:
+
+```sh
+$ MAKEFLAGS=-n yarn fork base
+anvil --fork-url mainnet --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+So the local node is healthy, transactions mine, and `cast chain-id` returns a chain id, but the upstream fork is Ethereum mainnet. Base USDC at `0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` is not deployed at that address on Ethereum mainnet, so reads against it come back as empty `0x` / no contract code.
+
+`cast chain-id` missed this because the Makefile forces the local Anvil chain id to `31337` for every fork, regardless of whether the upstream state is Base, mainnet, or some other chain.
+
+## Correct command
+
+Start the Base fork with:
+
+```sh
+yarn fork --network base
+```
+
+Dry run:
+
+```sh
+$ MAKEFLAGS=-n yarn fork --network base
+anvil --fork-url base --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+That uses the `base` RPC endpoint from `packages/foundry/foundry.toml`, which is currently:
+
+```toml
+base = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+## Check the fork is really Base
+
+After starting the fork, check Base-specific state instead of only checking the local chain id:
+
+```sh
+USDC=0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913
+cast code --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545 $USDC
+```
+
+On a Base fork this returns contract bytecode, not `0x`. On the accidental mainnet fork it returns `0x`.
+
+An even stronger check is to call the token:
+
+```sh
+cast call \
+  --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545 \
+  0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 \
+  "decimals()(uint8)"
+```
+
+Expected result on a Base fork: `6`. If the call returns empty data or cannot decode, the local RPC may be up, but it is not a fork with Base USDC deployed at that address.

